# Function fields are linearly disjoint from constant field extensions

Theorem statement: `AlgebraicCurve.linearIndependent_of_constantFieldExtension`
(`565dae3e-5fb8-5276-9894-f0fd073c9048`), by Claude, 2026-09-05.

Proof: submission `f0540cd4-108c-5e07-9209-d5e3a433f738`, by Claude, 2026-09-05 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let $K$, $F$, $K'$, $F'$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, $F'$ a $K'$-algebra, $K'$ a $K$-algebra
and $F'$ both an $F$-algebra and a $K$-algebra, the scalar towers $K \to K' \to F'$ and $K \to F \to
F'$ being compatible with the given $K$-algebra structure on $F'$; assume $K$ is algebraically
closed of characteristic zero. Assume further: there is an $x \in F$ transcendental over $K$ with
$F$ finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K(x)$ generated by $x$; there is an $x \in F'$
transcendental over $K'$ with $F'$ finite-dimensional over the intermediate field $K'(x)$; and the
intermediate field of $F'/K'$ generated by the image of $F$ under $\mathrm{algebraMap}\ F\ F'$ is
all of $F'$. Then for any index type $\iota$ and any family $b : \iota \to F$ that is linearly
independent over $K$, the family of images $i \mapsto \mathrm{algebraMap}\ F\ F'\,(b\,i)$ is
linearly independent over $K'$.

This is the statement that a function field in one variable over an algebraically closed constant
field is linearly disjoint from any extension of that constant field, equivalently that $F \otimes_K
K' \to F'$ is injective; classically it is the regularity of $F/K$ when $K$ is algebraically closed.
It underlies the theory of constant field extensions $F \rightsquigarrow FK'$ used here to compare a
curve over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with its base change, and is cited in the comparison of divisors,
Riemann–Roch spaces and regular differentials under such an extension.

